        "date": "1967-07-01T00:00:00Z",
        "description": "This article examines Franco Grignani’s experimental approach to graphic design, rooted in his architectural training at the Polytechnic of Turin. Influenced by Futurism and Constructivism, Grignani developed a distinctive visual language centered on optical effects and “visual ambiguity.” Active at Studio Boggeri after World War II, he merged scientific inquiry with artistic experimentation in both commercial and personal work. His dynamic optical paintings and bold advertising for clients such as Pirelli and Alfieri & Lacroix challenge perception, establishing his lasting influence on modern graphic design.",
